        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Oceania</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Māori</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> (Aotearoa), above all the North Island; the Archaic site of Wairau Bar in the South Island, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otago">Otago</a> and the mouth of the Waitaki River; Waitangi; the Chatham Islands (Rēkohu), home of the related Moriori.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Settlement c. 1250–1350; Archaic period c. 1300–1500; Classic period c. 1500–1769; early European contact 1769–1840; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a>, 1840; a living people today.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">voyaging waka and whakapapa (genealogy); iwi, hapū and whānau; tapu, noa and mana; pounamu (greenstone) weapons and ornaments; carved war canoes and wharenui (meeting houses); fortified pā; the haka; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-waitangi">Treaty of Waitangi</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Archaeology (Wairau Bar, moa-hunting sites, pā); radiocarbon dating and the Kaharoa Tephra; DNA and language studies; Māori oral history and genealogies; the journals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> and other European visitors.</span></div>
