    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The ancestors of the Māori sailed from eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> in ocean-going waka and settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> around 1250–1350, last of all the Polynesian lands.</li><li>In isolation they built a distinct culture: the moa-hunting Archaic period gave way after 1500 to the Classic culture of iwi and hapū, pounamu, carved meeting houses and fortified pā.</li><li>After European contact Māori traded and adopted new tools and writing, but suffered war, disease and the loss of most of their land; today they lead a strong revival of their language and culture.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When missionaries met Māori in 1815, Māori could draw accurate maps of their tribal boundaries on paper that matched European maps; the missionaries guessed that they had traditionally drawn maps on sand.</p></aside>
    </section>

